{"type": "thinking", "text": "\"15 hex in dec\" → 240. Expected 21 (0x15 = 21). 240 = 15*16. So the hex source value is scaled by 16! Confirms: each radix unit stored with radix as scaling factor, so any input unit value is multiplied by its radix.\n\nSo \"15 hex in dec\" = 15*16 = 240 instead of 21. That's the same bug pattern for all radices. Great, one root cause.\n\nAlso test binary input: \"10 bin in dec\" → expected 2, probably 20? Scaling by 2? Let's see: \"10 bin in dec\" maybe 10*2=20 → displayed 20. Hmm, actually scaling factor radix for bin is 2, so result 20. Let me verify quickly to characterize. Actually not necessary; report already confirmed. But more evidence strengthens. Skip.\n\nNow test decimal output of hex formatting maybe correct. We saw 0x96 for 150 correct. So output conversion seems right; input scaling is the issue.\n\nWait — actually maybe the output is also affected but cancels. E.g. \"15 dec in hex\": input 15 dec scaled by 10 = 150; output to hex maybe divided by 16? No, 150 in hex = 0x96. If output also scaled, would be different. So only input side scaled. And \"15 hex in dec\": input 15 hex scaled by 16 = 240, output dec unscaled = 240. Consistent: input unit scaling only.\n\nHmm, but wait: the units: \"dec\" unit radix 10 scaling factor 10; \"hex\" radix 16 scaling factor 16. What about the output unit \"hex\"? \"15 dec in hex\" gave 0x96. If output hex scaling by 16, result would be 150 -> 0x96 display. Hmm, what does \"in hex\" do? It converts 150 to base 16 = 0x96. Not scaled. So output formatting is fine.\n\nOK. Now let me exit scratchpad (doesn't save, so fine). Actually closing may discard. Let me press Back.\n\nNow let's test Date gaps. Press Back on scratchpad → home. Then open Date gaps.\n\nBut before that, note that scratchpad data is temporary; no persistence needed.\n\nLet me press Back."}
