{"type": "thinking", "text": "date(2024,2,29) to date(2025,2,28) = \"11 mo 30 d\". Is that correct? From Feb 29 2024 to Feb 28 2025 = 365 days. 11 months 30 days if month period Feb 29 + 11 months = Jan 29 2025, +30 days = Feb 28 2025. That accounts for 365 days. Alternatively 1 year exactly. Since end is Feb 28 (one day before anniversary), \"11 mo 30 d\" is defensible. OK.\n\nNow test datetime with time and month boundary:\ndatetime(2024, 1, 31, 23, 0, 0) to datetime(2024, 2, 29, 1, 0, 0): expected? Jan31 23:00 + 1 month = Feb 29 23:00 (clamp), but end is Feb 29 01:00 which is before, so 0 months? Hmm this can get weird. Let's not go down that rabbit hole unless there's a known bug.\n\nThe changed feature \"calendar periods are now computed directly up to the end timestamp\" — maybe the bug is that months/days are computed but time component now wrong. Let's test a case: datetime(2024, 1, 1, 10, 0, 0) to datetime(2024, 1, 2, 9, 0, 0) should be \"1 d\" if time becomes negative? No, it's 23 hours. Could be \"0 d 23 h\". Let's test to see if it shows \"1 d -1 h\" bug (which would be a wrong value). Actually the given examples all had positive time components. Let me test a case where time-of-day decreases and day difference is 1: 2024-01-01 10:00 → 2024-01-02 09:00 = 23 h. If algorithm computes days then subtracts, could produce weird. Let's test.\n\ndatetime(2024, 1, 1, 10, 0, 0) to datetime(2024, 1, 2, 9, 0, 0). Expected \"23 h\" or \"0 d 23 h\" or \"1 d -1 h\" (bug). Let me test.\n\nAlso test 2024-01-31 10:00 to 2024-03-01 09:00: expected? Let's first do the simple 23h case."}
