gitimately not support arbitrary time zones (maybe only \"UTC\" or offsets). Let me test the offset format to determine.\n\nLet me test \"UTC+5:30\". If that also gives 5h30m (i.e., ignored), then maybe only literal \"UTC\" is supported; but then passing \"Asia/Kolkata\" should perhaps error. Hmm.\n\nLet me check the app's help/docs. There's \"What's New\" and maybe Settings. Or the suggestions strip when typing function name might show signature. Let me type \"datetimeZ(\" and see suggestions. Actually the suggestion strip at top shows units and functions. When I typed \"datetimeZ(...\", the strip showed maybe \"CONV to/in/as\". Not helpful.\n\nLet me look at \"What's New\" from home. But that may be a changelog, could reveal features.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I'm overthinking. The app is NerdCalci — an open-source app. The datetimeZ function signature might accept timezone as an IANA zone. Let me test if the app shows an error for invalid zone like \"Foo/Bar\". If it accepts and ignores, likely bug.\n\nLet me test \"datetimeZ(2024,1,1,10,0,0,\"UTC\") to datetimeZ(2024,1,1,10,0,0,\"Foo/Bar\")\". If returns 0, zone ignored. Hmm.\n\nActually, a cleaner test: same wall time, different zone. datetimeZ(2024,1,1,10,0,0,\"UTC\") to datetimeZ(2024,1,1,5,0,0,\"UTC-05:00\"). Expected 0. Let me try \"UTC-5\" or \"UTC-05:00\".\n\nLet me try \"UTC-05:00\". Type it.\n\nActually, the bug is likely that timezone arg is ignored entirely (only used when \"UTC\"?). Let me test \"EST\" maybe. Ugh.\n\nLet me step back and consider what a QA would reasonably do: The precondition only includes \"UTC\". The changed build only mentions date range calc. The expected behavior of date/time functions with arbitrary IANA zones is not specified. Reporting a timezone bug might be rejected as not a bug if the syntax isn't supported. But the app silently accepting and ignoring is suspicious. However, \"a quote that was never on screen gets the report rejected\" — we have a quote \"-5 h\" which was on screen. The report would be about wrong value. The developer would reply.\n\nHmm, but they may say \"unsupported input syntax\". Let me test the offset form to increase confidence. Let me test \"UTC+05:30\".\n\nI'll replace with same instant using +05:30 offset."}
