{"type": "thinking", "text": "Bare \"32 fahrenheit\" → \"32 °F\". Correct, keeps unit. So the result unit for a single term is its own unit.\n\nNow \"32 fahrenheit + 32 fahrenheit\" → if result unit = first term's unit (°F), expected 64°F. If engine converts both to °C and displays °C, 0°C. Let's test. This will clarify.\n\nActually, \"25 celsius + 5 celsius\" gave 30°C, consistent with first unit. \"100 fahrenheit - 32 fahrenheit\" gave 37.78°C which is NOT first unit. So maybe when Fahrenheit is involved in arithmetic, it converts to Celsius? Let me test \"32 fahrenheit + 32 fahrenheit\".\n\nWait, maybe the issue: The unit of the result is determined by the operand that has an explicit unit and appears... Hmm. Actually maybe the expression parser uses the LAST unit keyword as the output unit? For \"100 fahrenheit - 32 fahrenheit\": last unit fahrenheit → should be °F. Not.\n\nMaybe it uses the FIRST operand's unit, but there's a bug in the new kelvin engine where Fahrenheit result is converted to Celsius. Hmm.\n\nLet's test \"32 fahrenheit + 32 fahrenheit\". If it yields 0°C, then there's a bug: same-unit Fahrenheit arithmetic displays Celsius (and computes converted). If 64°F, then the earlier result 37.78°C is... wait 100-32 = 68°F; converted to °C = 20°C, not 37.78. So 37.78 means it computed 37.78°C (=100°F) ignoring subtraction? No: 100°F minus 0°C = 37.78°C. So it DID subtract 32°F (=0°C) from 100°F (=37.78°C) and displayed 37.78°C. So arithmetic in °C. Good.\n\nFor \"32 fahrenheit + 32 fahrenheit\": 0°C + 0°C = 0°C. Let's test. If yes, then result unit is °C whenever arithmetic involves fahrenheit (i.e., the engine outputs celsius). But \"10 celsius + 18 fahrenheit\" → 2.22°C also. So maybe all arithmetic outputs °C by default. And \"25 celsius + 5 celsius\" → 30°C. So arithmetic always outputs °C. That's a plausible app design: temperature sums are always in Celsius.\n\nIf that's the design, then \"32 fahrenheit + 32 fahrenheit\" → 0°C is \"correct\" by design (sum of absolute temps in C). And not a bug.\n\nWe can't know. The precondition's given data all yield celsius results, consistent.\n\nI'll skip reporting temperature edge semantics.\n\nHmm, but the note \"Temperature unit engine: conversions are now computed through kelvin as the shared base unit\" — a bug might be that when adding Fahrenheit temps, the result is wrong. Let me compute \"10 celsius + 18 fahrenheit\" via a possible buggy kelvin implementation:\nIf each operand converted to kelvin, added, then converted back to °C: 283.15 + 265.3722 = 548.5222 → 275.37°C (not 2.22). App gives 2.22, so not buggy that way.\nIf converted to kelvin then subtracted offset for each before adding: (283.15-273.15) + (265.3722-273.15) = 10 + (-7.7778) = 2.2222 ✓. So engine converts to celsius deltas then adds, displaying celsius. This is the \"sum of values in celsius\" approach.\n\nThen \"50 fahrenheit + 10 celsius\": (10) + (10) = 20°C ✓.\n\"100 fahrenheit - 32 fahrenheit\": 37.78 - 0 = 37.78°C ✓.\n\"32 fahrenheit + 32 fahrenheit\": 0 + 0 = 0°C.\n\nSo the app's design: all temperature sums are in Celsius. Actually that's consistent with the file name \"Temperature sums\" and all results °C. The given third example confirms conversion to celsius before summing.\n\nOK so no bug there. Good. I'll not report.\n\nActually wait, that's a semantic choice: adding two Fahrenheit absolute temps and displaying Celsius. But the given example \"10 celsius + 18 fahrenheit\" → 2.22°C strongly implies sum-of-celsius. So yes.\n\nLet me move on.\n\nNow, let me think about the number bases bug more: The report is confirmed. Are there other number-base-related bugs? E.g., the input decimals scaling by 10 affects all conversions. Also maybe the display of octal/binary prefixes. Good enough.\n\nNow \"Home screen file list: files are now loaded in the background when the app launches.\" Let me test cold launch. To simulate: press HOME, then use open_app. But that resumes. Alternatively, from home screen press Back (exits app), then open_app. Let's try.\n\nFirst, leave scratchpad. Press Back to dismiss keyboard, then tap app Back. Wait, the scratchpad is open. To go home: press BACK (dismiss keyboard), then tap app element Back.\n\nActually I can just press HOME and reopen with open_app; the activity may be recreated? Not necessarily. Let me instead from home screen press device BACK which may finish the activity, then open_app.\n\nLet me do:\n1. Leave scratchpad → home.\n2. On home, press BACK (device) → app exits to launcher.\n3. open_app → relaunch, check list shows all 9 files.\n\nLet's do."}
