{"type": "thinking", "text": "\"32 fahrenheit + 32 fahrenheit\" → \"0 °C\". Hmm. So same-unit Fahrenheit addition gives Celsius zero. Is that wrong? 32°F + 32°F: if we add the numeric values in Fahrenheit, 64°F. The app converts each to Celsius (0 and 0) and adds → 0°C. So it doesn't preserve the unit. But is that the app's intended semantics? The \"Temperature sums\" file's results are °C. The app might deliberately compute sums in Celsius. But same-unit addition returning a different unit and a value that is not the sum of the numeric values (32+32=64) would surprise. Especially if the user wrote both in Fahrenheit.\n\nThis seems like a plausible bug introduced by the kelvin-based engine: summing in a shared base and displaying Celsius. But the given data's third line (10C + 18F = 2.22C) also sums in Celsius. So the engine's design is \"sum converted to Celsius\". Hmm.\n\nActually, wait. Let's revisit the given third line under the \"sum numeric values after converting to the first unit\" semantics:\n10°C + 18°F: convert 18°F to °C = -7.777; add to 10 = 2.222°C. Result unit °C ✓.\nAnd under \"sum in celsius\" semantics: same.\nSo both match.\n\nUnder \"sum in celsius\" for 32F+32F: 0+0 = 0°C.\nUnder \"first unit\" semantics: 32+32=64°F.\n\nWhich is the intended? The app's title \"Temperature sums\" and results in °C for the mixed example. If the intended were \"first unit\", then for \"10 celsius + 18 fahrenheit\" the first unit is celsius → °C ✓. So both again.\n\nThe only distinguishing case is F-first. We tested \"50 fahrenheit + 10 celsius\" → 20°C. Under first-unit semantics it'd be 100°F. Under celsius semantics 20°C. So the app uses celsius semantics (or converts to the LAST unit? no). If the design were \"first unit\", this would be a bug. But we don't know.\n\nGiven the file is named \"Temperature sums\" and all given results are °C, and the changed note says conversions through Kelvin (a shared base), it's plausible the intended result is always in Celsius or in the \"target\" unit. Hmm.\n\nActually, in NerdCalci, units are usually \"as-is\" unless you use \"in\". For plain numbers, \"5 km + 3 km = 8 km\" keeps unit. Would \"32 fahrenheit + 32 fahrenheit\" keep °F? Probably yes for a well-designed calculator. But the sum of absolute temperatures is dimensionally odd. Many calculators (e.g., NerdCalci) probably treat temperature addition as numeric addition after converting to the result unit (first term). If so, \"32F+32F=64°F\" and \"50F+10C=100°F\". The app giving 0°C and 20°C would be a bug.\n\nBut the given third line \"10 celsius + 18 fahrenheit\" = 2.22 °C would then be the result in celsius (first unit) ✓. So the app's bug would be that it fails to keep the first operand's unit for Fahrenheit (it always outputs Celsius). That's exactly the kind of regression a kelvin-base engine could cause: the result is always converted back to Celsius instead of the source unit.\n\nHowever, the given data doesn't reveal this. But the task says test edge cases. A QA could report this. The developer might confirm or say by-design. Given the risk, maybe report it. But earlier the date report first got \"could not reproduce\" then confirmed with better repro. So reporting a plausible bug could be fine.\n\nBut wait, is \"0 °C\" actually wrong under any reasonable spec? If the app outputs Celsius for temperature sums, then 32°F + 32°F = 0°C is mathematically \"sum of temperatures in Celsius\" (0+0). But the numeric sum of the inputs is not preserved. That's a weird but consistent spec. The file \"Temperature sums\" would then be understood as \"convert all to Celsius and sum\". The changed engine note says \"conversions are now computed through kelvin as the shared base unit\" — for a \"shared base\" they'd use Kelvin, not Celsius. Hmm, if the arithmetic were done in Kelvin (the shared base), then 32°F (273.15K) + 32°F (273.15K) = 546.3K = 273.15°C, not 0°C. So the app is NOT doing arithmetic in Kelvin. It converts to Celsius then adds. So the \"kelvin shared base\" is only for conversions.\n\nIf the intended arithmetic were \"add numeric values in the first unit\", the app would display °F. Since it displays °C, maybe the engine converts the operands to Celsius (via Kelvin) and doesn't convert back. That could be the seeded bug! But the given third line 10C+18F = 2.22°C is consistent with either.\n\nHmm. I need stronger evidence. Let me test \"50 kelvin + 50 kelvin\". If the app sums in Celsius, 50K = -223.15°C, sum = -446.3°C. If it keeps Kelvin, 100K. What does it show? If it shows -446.3°C, then definitely all sums are in Celsius. Is that plausible? The app might always output Celsius for temperature sums.\n\nI think the app's temperature sums are intended to be in Celsius OR maybe in the leftmost unit. Without docs, uncertain.\n\nLet me check the changelog for temperature mentions. Search may reveal intended behavior. Let me scroll What's New. It's long, but I can do maybe 10 swipes and scan. There might be an entry \"Added temperature unit conversion\" etc. The app's official docs might say \"Temperature sums are always in Celsius\" or \"in the first unit\".\n\nActually, maybe there's an in-app help/cheatsheet. The \"More options\" in the file editor might have \"Help\". Or the main Settings might have \"Syntax guide\". Let me check Settings. There might be a link to \"Documentation\".\n\nLet me check Settings quickly. From home, tap Settings (element 20). But I'm in scratchpad with keyboard. Let me exit.\n\nActually, the task is about QA, not documentation research. But understanding intended behavior matters.\n\nGiven the time spent, maybe I should prioritize: We have 2 confirmed bugs (number bases, date gaps). The task says sign off when areas have no remaining bugs. If there's a temperature bug, signing off would be bad. Let me investigate the temperature semantics a bit more via changelog/docs, then decide.\n\nLet me exit scratchpad and open Settings to look for Help/docs."}
