{"type": "thinking", "text": "Excellent! The Help documentation reveals the intended semantics!\n\nKey section 10 \"Unit conversions\":\n\"Dimension-safe arithmetic: When you mix different units (like adding meters to kilometers), NerdCalci automatically picks the most precise unit for the result. For temperature, it uses the unit from the right-hand side. You can always override this by adding `in <unit>` at the end:\n10km + 500m → 10500 m\n10km + 500m in km → 10.50 km\n35 °C + 10 degF → 105 °F\n35 °C + 10 degF in degC → 40.56 °C\"\n\nWhoa. So for temperature, the result uses the unit from the RIGHT-HAND side. Let's check \"35 °C + 10 degF → 105 °F\".\n35°C = 95°F. 95 + 10 = 105°F. So it converts the left to the right's unit (F) and adds the raw right value: 95°F + 10°F = 105°F. So the semantics: result unit = right-hand unit, and values are added as converted absolute temps? Let's see: 35°C = 95°F; + 10°F = 105°F. Yes.\n\nAnd \"35 °C + 10 degF in degC → 40.56 °C\": 105°F = 40.5556°C ✓ (105°F = 40.56°C).\n\nNow apply to our app's \"10 celsius + 18 fahrenheit\": RHS unit = fahrenheit. Convert 10°C to °F = 50°F. Add 18°F = 68°F. Then, with no \"in\", result should be \"68 °F\"! But the app showed \"2.22 °C\". That's wrong per the documentation!\n\nWait, but the precondition's \"Temperature sums\" third line expected maybe 2.22°C? The app displays 2.22°C. According to the docs, it should be 68 °F. So the \"Temperature sums\" file's third line is WRONG due to the regression!\n\nHold on, but the test file \"Temperature sums\" was presumably created with the app's intended behavior. The precondition only gives inputs, not outputs. So the correct expected for \"10 celsius + 18 fahrenheit\" is 68 °F per the docs. The app shows 2.22 °C. That's a bug!\n\nLet me also verify \"35 °C + 10 degF\" in the app should give 105 °F. Let me test. And \"35 °C + 10 degF in degC\" should give 40.56 °C.\n\nThis is the temperature engine bug! The result unit should be the right-hand unit, but the app seems to always output Celsius.\n\nLet's verify the app's given Temperature sums lines:\n1. \"25 celsius + 5 celsius\" RHS celsius → 30 °C ✓ (both same).\n2. \"20 celsius - 5 celsius\" → 15 °C ✓.\n3. \"10 celsius + 18 fahrenheit\" → RHS fahrenheit → expected 68 °F. App shows 2.22 °C. WRONG.\n\nSo the third line is a bug. Great. Let me confirm with the doc example \"35 °C + 10 degF\" → expect 105 °F. Let me test in scratchpad. Also \"35 °C + 10 degF in degC\" → 40.56 °C.\n\nAlso the doc says \"For temperature, it uses the unit from the right-hand side.\" So our \"50 fahrenheit + 10 celsius\" → RHS celsius → convert 50°F to 10°C, add 10 = 20°C ✓ (app showed 20°C, correct!). And \"32 fahrenheit + 32 fahrenheit\" → RHS fahrenheit → 32+32 = 64°F, but app showed 0°C, wrong! And \"100 fahrenheit - 32 fahrenheit\" → RHS fahrenheit → 100-32 = 68°F, app showed 37.78°C, wrong.\n\nSo the bug: when the right-hand unit is Fahrenheit (or Kelvin?), the result is wrongly output in Celsius and computed after converting the left to Celsius. Effectively, it always converts to Celsius and outputs Celsius, instead of converting to the RHS unit.\n\nWait, for \"50 fahrenheit + 10 celsius\", RHS celsius: expected 20°C (app correct). For \"10 celsius + 18 fahrenheit\", RHS fahrenheit: expected 68°F (app wrong, shows 2.22°C). So the bug is specifically when the RHS unit is non-Celsius (Fahrenheit/Kelvin). The engine computes in Celsius and never converts the result back to the RHS unit.\n\nThis matches \"Temperature unit engine: conversions are now computed through kelvin as the shared base unit\" — a regression where the result unit handling broke.\n\nLet me verify with the exact doc examples. \"35 °C + 10 degF\" should be 105 °F. Let me test. And \"35 °C + 10 degF in degC\" → 40.56 °C (this would be correct even with the bug because explicit \"in degC\"). Let me test both. If \"35 °C + 10 degF\" shows 40.56 °C, that's the bug.\n\nAlso test \"32 fahrenheit + 32 fahrenheit\" → expected 64 °F; app showed 0 °C. Confirmed bug already.\n\nSo I should report this temperature bug. The \"Temperature sums\" file line 3 is the in-scope evidence: \"10 celsius + 18 fahrenheit\" should show 68 °F per docs (RHS unit Fahrenheit), but shows \"2.22 °C\".\n\nHmm wait, but let me double check the doc example math: \"35 °C + 10 degF → 105 °F\". If RHS is degF, result in °F. 35°C = 95°F. 95 + 10 = 105°F ✓. Yes.\n\nSo for \"10 celsius + 18 fahrenheit\": 10°C = 50°F; 50 + 18 = 68°F. Expected 68 °F.\n\nLet me verify with the app to be sure by entering the doc example. Let me go to scratchpad and type \"35 °C + 10 degF\". But \"°C\" symbol input might be tricky; the app supports \"degF\" and \"degC\". Let me use \"35 degC + 10 degF\" (since \"°C\" typing might be hard; but the doc uses 35 °C). The precondition uses \"celsius\"/\"fahrenheit\" words. Let me test \"35 celsius + 10 fahrenheit\" → expected 105 °F.\n\nYes! 35°C = 95°F; +10 = 105°F. Let me test. If app shows 40.56 °C, bug confirmed (in addition to the file line).\n\nActually the file line itself is enough: \"10 celsius + 18 fahrenheit\" shows \"2.22 °C\" but expected \"68 °F\". Let me file the report against the Temperature sums file, element 8 (result \"2.22 °C\"), quote \"2.22 °C\". Expected 68 °F per the Help docs (RHS unit). Steps: open Temperature sums, read line 3.\n\nBut I should be careful: is the expected \"68 °F\" or \"68 fahrenheit\"? The docs show \"105 °F\" (with symbol). So expected \"68 °F\".\n\nLet me also confirm with the doc example in scratchpad to strengthen. But I'm confident. Let me first verify with the app maybe \"35 celsius + 10 fahrenheit\" → should be 105 °F. Let me do a quick test in scratchpad (it's currently empty? we're in Help now). Actually we're in the Help screen. Let me go back to home then scratchpad and test. But I already have \"32°F+32°F = 0°C\" evidence (expected 64°F). And the file line.\n\nLet me get the file evidence fresh: Go back to Help → Back (Help&feedback) → Back (Settings) → home → open Temperature sums. Or I can file against the scratchpad \"32 fahrenheit + 32 fahrenheit\" quote \"0 °C\". That's on record but the scratchpad was reset; not currently on screen. The file Temperature sums is a stable in-scope screen.\n\nLet me navigate to Temperature sums and file.\n\nAlso, I should test \"35 celsius + 10 fahrenheit\" to match the docs exactly, to be safe. Let me do it in scratchpad quickly, then go to the file.\n\nActually, the file line is exactly the same pattern (celsius + fahrenheit). The docs make the expected clear. Let me test the doc example to be thorough. From Help, press Back → Help&feedback, Back → Settings, Back → home. Then scratchpad.\n\nLet me do it."}
