{"type": "thinking", "text": "\"35 degC + 10 degF in degF\" → \"73 °F\". But the Help says \"35 °C + 10 degF → 105 °F\" and \"35 °C + 10 degF in degC → 40.56 °C\". 105°F = 40.56°C. The app's \"in degF\" gives 73°F (=22.78°C). So the arithmetic itself is wrong: it converts the right operand 10°F to -12.22°C and adds to 35 = 22.78°C = 73°F. It should instead convert the left 35°C to 95°F and add 10 = 105°F.\n\nSo the app's semantics is \"convert both to a common base (Celsius) and add\" rather than \"convert left to right's unit and add\". The Help says the latter. The changed note \"conversions are now computed through kelvin as the shared base unit\" — perhaps they changed the arithmetic to convert both to the shared base (kelvin/celsius) and add, which is wrong. This is THE temperature engine bug.\n\nNow I have a very clear repro:\n- \"35 degC + 10 degF in degF\" → 73 °F. Expected 105 °F (documented example).\nThis also shows it's not a display-unit issue; the numeric result is wrong.\n\nThis is a strong bug report. The dev's previous rejection might be because the file's 2.22°C could be interpreted as a celsius-sum convention. But the doc example with explicit \"in degF\" → 73°F vs expected 105°F is unambiguous.\n\nLet me refile with this. Use the current screen (scratchpad) element 40 \"73 °F\". Expected 105 °F per Help example. Steps reference the doc.\n\nLet me file."}
{"type": "text", "text": "Now it's clear the arithmetic itself is wrong: \"35 degC + 10 degF in degF\" gives 73 °F, but the Help example expects 105 °F. Let me report this precise repro."}
{"type": "tool_call", "callId": "call_00_hICfFZJF6hd9nubwfdI36079", "tool": "mcp__device__file_report", "input": {"element": "Result of scratchpad expression \"35 degC + 10 degF in degF\"", "element_ref": 40, "expected": "105 °F. The in-app Help (Settings > Help & feedback > Help > \"10. Unit conversions\") explicitly gives \"35 °C + 10 degF → 105 °F\" and \"35 °C + 10 degF in degC → 40.56 °C\". The left operand must be converted to the right operand's unit (35°C = 95°F) and then 10°F added = 105°F.", "observed": "\"73 °F\" is shown. The engine instead converts the right-hand Fahrenheit value into Celsius (10°F = -12.22°C) and adds it to 35°C, producing 22.78°C (73°F). This happens even with an explicit \"in degF\", so the computed value itself is wrong, not just the displayed unit. Same root cause makes Temperature sums line 3 \"10 celsius + 18 fahrenheit\" show \"2.22 °C\" instead of 68 °F.", "screen": "Scratchpad (temporary file)", "steps": ["Open Settings > Help & feedback > Help > section 10 'Unit conversions' (documents '35 °C + 10 degF → 105 °F')", "Open the temporary scratchpad", "Enter: 35 degC + 10 degF in degF", "Observe the result '73 °F' instead of the documented 105 °F", "Also check Temperature sums line 3 '10 celsius + 18 fahrenheit' -> '2.22 °C' (should be 68 °F)"], "symptom_type": "wrong_value", "observed_quote": "73 °F"}}
